## Required source deliverables

- [ ] Complete PMK/PTK derivation, nonce generation, EAPOL MIC/replay checks,
      the four-way handshake, AES unwrap and GTK rekey with explicit bounds,
      state transitions, timeouts and key erasure on every failure path.
- [ ] Implement actual CCMP protection/deprotection or verified hardware
      offload, keycache programming/removal and TX/RX packet-number lifetime.
      Handle retransmissions, replay, group traffic and connection epochs.
- [ ] Wire cryptographic callbacks to real implementations and packet/key
      consumers. A codec, callback declaration or handshake state enum alone
      does not implement encrypted packet delivery.
- [ ] Keep the controlled data port closed until the accepted handshake and
      keys are installed. Preserve EAPOL access required by authentication;
      disconnect/reset/sleep cannot retain stale usable keys or reuse a PN.
- [ ] Record static algorithm/specification comparisons and reviewed boundary
      cases with necessary offline regressions, without claiming certification.

## Start here and next implementation step

Wpa2Supplicant::onMessage1/onMessage3 handles the initial exchange using
Wpa2CryptoAdapter and portable Wpa2Primitives. Eapol.* supplies parsing/replay
logic. KeyHardware is still a native callback contract; no AR9565 hardware
key installer is connected. `src/wifi/crypto/Ccmp.{hpp,cpp}` now implements
source-only AES-CCM-128/8-byte MIC and 802.11 CCMP IV/AAD/nonce for bounded
three-address non-QoS and QoS Data MPDUs, nonfragmented and without FCS.
QoS nonce priority and 24-byte AAD carry the authenticated TID; replay checks
are per TID, sharing a floor for legacy data and QoS TID 0, while transmit PN
is monotonically owned per TK. Its serialized `CcmpSession` rejects wrong
epochs/transmitters, ambiguous buffer aliases, A-MSDU/HT-control/fragmented
frames, stale per-TID receive PNs, wrong key IDs, exhausted transmit PN and
failed authentication without disclosing decrypted bytes or advancing replay
state. It erases ephemeral round keys and private session key on
revoke/destruction. Offline tests include independent OpenSSL AES-CCM non-QoS
and QoS ciphertext/MIC vectors, QoS out-of-order TIDs, tampering/replay,
masked mutable QoS controls and long-payload round trips. The
software AES lookup-table implementation is **not timing-audited** and must
not be enabled on an exposed shared kernel without cryptographic side-channel
review or a constant-time native/hardware alternative. The portable data path
now has an explicit CCMP-protected transmit API for ordinary WPA2 Ethernet-II
traffic and supplicant-authenticated Group2; the existing cleartext serializer
still refuses Group2. The native PCI owner now calls the protected TX
serializer and queues its software-encrypted MPDU with hardware cipher mode
Clear. This is **not** a live authorized Tahoe network data port: keycache,
OS supplicant ownership, protected RX and regulatory RF setup remain open.
Inbound Group1 is admitted only when CCMP-verified and key-installed.
`WifiEngine::acceptProtectedGroupRekey` now connects this bounded RX
admission, the supplicant's authenticated Group1/GTK update, and
the pairwise-CCMP-protected Group2 response. This is a portable driver-owned
API, not a native registered RX/TX callback. Owner-driven PN persistence and
Tahoe integration remain open.

The portable `Wpa2Supplicant` now retains only KCK/KEK after M4 and handles
post-install Group Key Handshake message 1 through an explicit API. It checks
EAPOL length, descriptor type, MIC, monotonic group replay counter, CCMP Key
RSC, wrapped GTK KDE and callback mutation/reentrancy. It signs Group2 with
KCK and attempts the GTK/RSC installation before returning the response.
Offline synthetic C++14/C++17 sanitizer regressions cover successful rekeys,
replay, corrupted MIC, bad KDE/RSC, partial install failure, tampering and
revocation. These callbacks are fake; no hardware key operation is proven.

The native OS-owned WPA2 receive owner now routes group-addressed CCMP
Key ID **zero** to its dedicated GTK slot, rather than rejecting it or
misrouting it to pairwise PTK zero. The two-bit on-air Key ID permits 0..3;
unicast still requires pairwise index zero, and the session independently
checks the authenticated MIC and replay/PN before delivery. A source-backed
CCMP group-zero vector regression covers key selection, valid decrypt,
wrong-slot rejection and replay. This corrects group address/key selection
only; it does not authorize RF, prove native OS key installation, or complete
protected Group1/Group2 rekey integration.

**Driver-owned native Group1/Group2 source integration is now wired, but
physical operation is unverified.** The existing portable
`WifiEngine::acceptProtectedGroupRekey` still supports a complete single-call
CCMP Group1/MIC/replay/GTK/Group2 exchange. A new
`WifiEngine::finishAuthenticatedGroupRekey` accepts only the already-decoded,
single-MIC/PN-verified Group1 `Msdu` from the native RX owner. It checks the
live driver-owned association, selected AP, station destination, RX trust,
replay/MIC and exact EAPOL Group1, then authenticates the GTK/RSC via the
supplicant and emits a pairwise-CCMP-protected Group2 with the native owner's
MAC sequence. This avoids decrypting the same Group1 twice or using a stale
portable TX sequence. Native `SimpleATH::receiveNativeProtected` binds the
verified borrowed plaintext to the real keycache callback, requires exclusive
`NativeQueues::beginKeyUpdateGated()` isolation and defers Group2 until the
receive DMA borrower unwinds and the same-epoch queues physically restart.
Group2 is submitted with software-protected descriptor mode, tracked by its
**own exact EDMA completion token**, never confused with the initial M4
port-opening token. GTK/key/association state is revoked if preparation,
restart/submission or Group2 completion fails. Existing WPA2 M2/M4 cannot
transmit Group2 in cleartext.

Driver-owned and OS-owned WPA2 remain mutually exclusive. The Tahoe WCL v2
native connect selects the driver supplicant when supplied a private PMK;
any OS-owned key path must retain its own supplicant and protected Group2
handling, not invoke the new driver-owned completion API. The source also
contains a separately scoped OS key installer and protected EAPOL routing;
those source paths are **not** proof that macOS actually supplied a PMK, that
RF and EDMA ran or that an AP accepted Group2. Hardware RX ciphertext versus
automatic decrypt/keycache handling, same-epoch RF activation, regulatory/TPC
authority, AP rekey timing and on-device retry/timeout validation still need
independent evidence before declaring operational network rekeying. New
synthetic C++14 and C++17 ASan/UBSan fixtures cover the double-decrypt
avoidance, native-sequence Group2 success, replay, missing RX trust, output
alias and failed encryption rollback. No new kext build or physical test.

Connect the existing guarded AR9565 keycache programming/removal with the
source-only CCMP packet engine under a single native epoch, then complete
group-key rekey, retry/timeouts and owner reset/rollback. Never reuse a TK
with a reset transmit PN, including sleep/reset/handover and failed M4. Bind
message processing to the same immutable EAPOL snapshot, BSS RSNE and connection
epoch. Treat cipher/decrypt/MIC/PN status as authenticated input before payload
delivery. Define what happens if M2/M4 encoding/submission or any key operation
fails; erase secrets and revoke callbacks before their contexts are released.

Before wiring the supplicant into native AirPort, resolve with A8 whether the
OS or this source owns EAPOL, key derivation and controlled-port authorization.
Route each key operation through one connection owner and generation; do not
run two independent handshakes. Diagnostics must never log PMK/PTK/GTK or other
secret key material.

KeyHardware::secureRandom still needs an eligible Tahoe kernel CSPRNG and an
explicit failure contract; timestamps, counters or synthetic test nonces are
never production entropy. The current install callbacks are synchronous and
receive borrowed key bytes. A future asynchronous installer must own an
erasable copy with a connection token and report actual installation before
opening the data port; retaining the callback's stack pointer is invalid.
Resolve M4 submission/completion failure without leaving a falsely authorized
connection or reinstalling the same key/PN on a duplicate M3.

The required baseline is WPA2-PSK with CCMP on the supported 2.4 GHz station
path. Explicitly reject unsupported AKM/cipher/PMF requirements and never
advertise them as implemented. WPA3, enterprise authentication and unrelated
legacy ciphers are not substitutes for completing this baseline.

Use published IEEE 802.11/RSN behavior and the existing crypto design record.
For hardware key layout consult ath9k
[key.c](https://github.com/torvalds/linux)
and register definitions, with file-specific notices retained where applicable.
Do not confuse message codecs with the radio's installed-key state.

Useful host regressions: known algorithm vectors, stale replay counters,
duplicate
M3 without key/PN reinstall, mismatched RSNE, malformed wrapped GTK/padding,
nonce/output overlap, group RSC/PN propagation, timeout and failed key rollback.
Use synthetic keys only; passing vectors is not cryptographic certification.

